   // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
   push(H1('1.  Introduction', { pageBreakBefore: true }));
   push(P0(`The quantum Yang–Mills problem of the Clay Mathematics Institute is a single sentence. For every compact simple gauge group *G* it asks for a proof that a non-trivial quantum Yang–Mills theory exists on ℝ⁴ and has a mass gap Δ > 0.[[fn:${S.clay}, §4, p. 6.]] The sentence joins two demands of different kinds. The first is a construction: a system of correlation functions, free of every cutoff, defined on the whole of space-time and obeying axioms at least as strong as those of Wightman and of Osterwalder and Schrader. The second is an inequality: the spectrum of the Hamiltonian of the theory so constructed must leave an interval above zero empty. The inequality cannot be stated before the construction exists, and when the construction proceeds by compactness, the problem's own text, in a footnote examined in Section 3, does not count it as complete until properties of this kind are proved for the limit.`));
-  push(P(`In two dimensions the continuum Yang–Mills measure was constructed in 1989, by Driver and by Gross, King and Sengupta.[[fn:B. K. Driver, "YM₂: continuum expectations, lattice convergence, and lassos", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 123 (1989); L. Gross, C. King and A. Sengupta, "Two dimensional Yang–Mills theory via stochastic differential equations", *Ann. Physics* 194 (1989).]] In three and in four dimensions Bałaban controlled Wilson's lattice theory on a torus by a renormalisation group whose bounds are uniform in the lattice spacing.[[fn:T. Bałaban, "Ultraviolet stability of three-dimensional lattice pure gauge field theories", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 102 (1985), 255–275; "Renormalization group approach to lattice gauge field theories. I", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 109 (1987), 249–301; and the further papers of the series, *Comm. Math. Phys.* 95–122 (1984–1989).]] When the problem was posed, its authors wrote that a detailed construction on a compact space would be a major breakthrough, and that even then "no present ideas point the direction to establish the existence of a mass gap that is uniform in the volume. Nor do present methods suggest how to obtain the existence of the infinite volume limit T⁴ → ℝ⁴."[[fn:${S.clay}, §6, p. 7.]]`));
+  push(P(`In two dimensions the continuum Yang–Mills measure was constructed in 1989, by Driver and by Gross, King and Sengupta.[[fn:B. K. Driver, "YM₂: continuum expectations, lattice convergence, and lassos", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 123 (1989); L. Gross, C. King and A. Sengupta, "Two dimensional Yang–Mills theory via stochastic differential equations", *Ann. Physics* 194 (1989).]] In three dimensions Bałaban controlled Wilson's lattice theory on a torus by a renormalisation group whose bounds are uniform in the lattice spacing; in four dimensions he proved the same bounds under the hypothesis that the running couplings stay below an uncomputed constant at every scale, a hypothesis whose own proof his paper of record states to be unpublished (§8.3).[[fn:T. Bałaban, "Ultraviolet stability of three-dimensional lattice pure gauge field theories", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 102 (1985), 255–275; "Renormalization group approach to lattice gauge field theories. I", *Comm. Math. Phys.* 109 (1987), 249–301; and the further papers of the series, *Comm. Math. Phys.* 95–122 (1984–1989).]] When the problem was posed, its authors wrote that a detailed construction on a compact space would be a major breakthrough, and that even then "no present ideas point the direction to establish the existence of a mass gap that is uniform in the volume. Nor do present methods suggest how to obtain the existence of the infinite volume limit T⁴ → ℝ⁴."[[fn:${S.clay}, §6, p. 7.]]`));
   push(P(`On 6 October 2026 Ethan Sussman opened a public repository for the joint work of Levent Alpöge and himself, whose stated aim is "to induce LLMs to develop, push, and extend Balaban's program to construct Yang--Mills_4 (YM4) and other quantum gauge theories".[[fn:${S.readme}]] It now holds an 854-page manuscript on three-dimensional SU(2) Yang–Mills theory, a revised edition of 856 pages, a 1,044-page sequel on curvature bilinears, a 263-page manuscript on three-dimensional quantum electrodynamics, a 30-page manuscript on rotation invariance from reflection positivity, and a 153-page zeroth draft on four-dimensional SU(2) Yang–Mills theory.[[fn:${S.counts}]] Peter Woit discussed the repository on 26 October 2026.[[fn:${S.woit}]] Its standing has since been compressed into a single sentence: a public three-dimensional manuscript proving the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms for tr F², about 854 pages, "LLM-approved", unverified, with subsequential infinite-volume limits and no gap; and a four-dimensional existence argument that its authors call extremely long and not currently understandable, being rewritten slowly.`));
   push(P(`This paper replaces that sentence by a proof. Each of its clauses is unfolded into a precise mathematical statement, each statement is traced to the sentence of the primary source that carries it, and the consequences for the Millennium problem are deduced. The outcome is the Ground Theorem of Section 2. Its first three parts establish what the corpus asserts in three dimensions, what it asserts in four, and on what standing it asserts either. Its fourth part establishes what the corpus leaves undone and proves that one inequality completes it: a lower bound on the lattice mass gap, uniform in physical units along the construction's own sequence of lattices. That inequality is the object on which the proof that follows will stand.`));
   push(P(`Quotations are verbatim, including the authors' spelling. Page counts were measured by counting the page breaks in the text extracted from each file. Every source was read on 28 October 2026; the repository's most recent commit is dated 27 October 2026, 10:45:28 UTC, and its README was last changed on 26 October 2026, 16:18:10 UTC. The deductions of Sections 7 and 8 assume nothing about the correctness of the corpus. They hold for every construction of the shape the corpus describes.`));
