+  push(P(`Hypothesis I is read on the effective action of the coarse variables. Write the blocked measure as λ_{j}(*dv*) = *e*^{−A}*dv* with respect to the product over the blocks of a fixed reference measure on *V*, so that *A* = *A*_{j}(*v*) is the effective action at the coarse scale, and let Δ_{yy′}(*A*) be its mixed second difference in the sense of Proposition 8.4: the supremum, over coarse configurations differing only at *y*′, of the oscillation in *v*_{y} of the difference of the two actions. The conditional law of *v*_{y} under one of the two configurations is the tilt of its conditional law under the other by that difference, so the first inequality of Proposition 8.4 gives *C*^{λ}_{j}(*y*, *y*′) ≤ 1 − *e*^{−Δ} with Δ = Δ_{yy′}(*A*). Hypothesis I is therefore a bound on the mixed second differences of the effective action between blocks, weighted by their distance: the influence functional of §8.1, and the number that a certificate of the second link must deliver at the coarse scale.`, { before: 120 }));
+  push(P(`One might wish to state the hypothesis on the fine measure directly, taking the blocks as the sites of a Gibbs measure and the whole configuration of a block as its variable, so that Dobrushin's condition for that measure would give every local observable's decay at once, with no fibre clause. That form is not uniform in the cutoff. Its interdependence coefficient between adjacent blocks is the total variation between two laws of all the links of a block under outside data that differ on a whole neighbouring face; as the spacing tends to zero that face carries ever more links, the two laws become distinguishable almost surely by an average over the face, and the coefficient tends to one at every coupling, so that the row sums tend to at least one. Whole-block total variation is the one norm that does not survive large blocks, and the hypothesis of the reduction is therefore stated on the coarse variables, whose type is fixed, with the field inside the blocks entering through its clustering alone.`, { before: 120 }));
+  push(P(`Theorem 8.5 is the second link in the form the route attacks it. Hypothesis I is a bound on the blocked measure at the coarse scale, the object named in §8.1 as the influence functional of the blocked action. Hypothesis II is what the ultraviolet half must deliver below that scale; for the gauge group SU(2) the literature states nothing in this form, so it stands beside Hypothesis I as the second half of what the crossover must supply, and it is named as such. Proposition 8.4 is the fourth link in its exact form. The blocked measures λ_{j} have variables of one type at every rung, so it is meaningful to say that their conditional laws at the coarse scale are tilts of those of one rung *j*₁ by functions δ_{j} whose oscillations *o*_{j} and weighted mixed differences Σ_{y′}(1 − *e*^{−Δ})*e*^{κ|y−y′|} tend to zero; when they are, Hypothesis I at the rung *j*₁ with the constant α₁ implies it at every later rung with any α ∈ (α₁, 1), by (8.7). That the measures stand in this relation is the ultraviolet interface of §8.1, and it is the place at which Bałaban's theorem enters the chain, with its two unpublished pieces and with the unpublished proof of its own hypothesis on the sequence of couplings.`));
+  push(P(`The theorem places the four-dimensional gap at one remove from a finite statement. Hypotheses I and II concern the conditional law of one coarse variable and the field inside one block, at a fixed physical scale, with constants uniform in the cutoff, and nothing in them refers to a limit; their consequence, by Theorem 8.5, is the unique vacuum, the cluster property and the mass gap of every limit point, with the gap's value in (8.8). The proof that follows this paper is the proof of those two statements, and its measure of progress is a number: the distance, in blocking steps, between the scale at which the ultraviolet half delivers its interface and the scale at which the blocked bound is read. On the hierarchical model of the renormalisation group, in which each blocking step is the Migdal–Kadanoff recursion on a single-plaquette weight and is exact for the hierarchical lattice, that distance has been measured: Edwin Boston III has certified the flow of the SU(2) Wilson weight at 256 bits of precision with directed rounding, every coefficient an interval and the truncation priced, and from β = 2.3 the fundamental character coefficient falls below the finite-group threshold 0.131 of §8.2 at the seventh step, from β = 5 at the eighteenth, and from β = 10 at the thirty-seventh.[[fn:Edwin Boston III, the certified descent; the instrument, its records at the four starting couplings and the run of record are listed in the Records. The first step from β = 2.2 is read from the same record: the coefficient rises from 0.464 to 0.486, an effective coupling of 2.34, where the one-loop running of ℤ⁴ takes 2.2 to 1.94 under one doubling of the scale.]] The record also says what the model is not. Its first step from β = 2.2 moves the coupling up where the running of ℤ⁴ moves it down, by an amount of the step's own size, so the model certifies the entry into the disk from its fifth rung and the threshold at its seventh, and not the crossover's first steps, which a flow