 ### 3, read at 04:4x EDT: the instrument cannot resolve its own stakes
 
 The two shorter runs closed (1,200 measured sweeps each, side 6; records `records/second-road/blockresponse_*_L6_short.txt`). The known answers held (S3: ⟨P⟩ = 0.60007 at β = 2.3; ⟨cos φ⟩ = 0.75223 at β = 1.2). The profiles did not read the physics: for both groups the mean absolute covariance J sits near 1 × 10⁻² at every distance from 2.25 to 5.25, falling by a factor of three over the whole range for SU(2) and for U(1) alike (fitted rates 0.21 and 0.25 per spacing), and that value is the floor of the estimator itself: the absolute value of a covariance estimated from N = 1,200 samples of two variables with spreads 0.3 and 0.5 has an expected magnitude near 0.3 × 0.5 / √N ≈ 4 × 10⁻³ even when the true covariance is zero. The sampler therefore reads its own noise beyond two lattice spacings, in both phases. S1, S2 and S4 are untested, not refuted; the longer runs, three times the samples, would lower the floor by a factor below two and were stopped. This is the operator's rule of 04:3x in a number: a sampling instrument at the crossover reads nothing a certificate could use, and here it does not even read the known physics. The page keeps the stakes as written and takes no reading from them.
+
+## §5 — Two exact readings of 06:03 by the clock (iii-38; evidence, not certificates)
+
+**Conjecture G's first exact four-dimensional test.** On the cube with positive boundary fields at β = 2.2 (caps (4, 3, 3), the spins-≤-1 model with nothing dropped) every loop-pair connected correlation is positive: adjacent ⟨χ_½χ_½⟩_c = +0.009109012 and ⟨χ₁χ_½⟩_c = +0.017862948, opposite +0.002937965 and +0.005756668 (`records/pentapylon/g31_pair_b2.2.2026-10-29a.txt`). The stake — adjacent positive, opposite smaller and positive — held; a negative beyond 1e-9 would have refuted Griffiths II over nonnegative character weights. 1.0 and 0.5 follow. Evidence for G; G's use (§2(C)) is still barred by the U(1) test until a Simon–Lieb form for untruncated loops is written and checked.
+
+**The one-step blocked coupling parts the groups.** The 2 × 2 loop on a four-dimensional object at the ordered datum, exact: for SU(2) at β = 2.2, W(2,2) = 0.315340 reads through the one-plaquette law as a plaquette at β_eff(2a) = 1.354 — the coarse coupling runs DOWN by the factor 0.62 in one blocking step — while for U(1) at β = 2.0, W(2,2) = 0.736 reads as a plaquette at 2.26, the ratio β_eff/β above 1 at every β above 0.7 (0.733 / 1.139 / 1.199 / 1.207 / 1.206 / 1.184 / 1.130 at 0.5 … 2.0). This is the discriminator §1 names — the running of the effective coupling under blocking, which SU(2) has and the Coulomb phase lacks — read for the first time on a four-dimensional object rather than the hierarchical model, and it passes the U(1) test by construction. Caveats, each carried into the record: the ordered datum (every outside link the identity; the random datum next), the spin-1 truncation of the SU(2) weight, and β_eff defined by the one-plaquette law applied to W(2,2). The iterate of the map is staked before its run: SU(2) from 2.2 below 0.2414 within six steps, monotone; U(1) from 2.0 never falling. A reading of the running is not the renormalisation step: the step is the inequality that carries Hypothesis I along it, still the object of §2(B).
