89), the two large-field papers (1989), the classical N-vector series (1995–1999), and the bosonic many-body series with Feldman, Knörrer and Trubowitz (2008–2022); no paper on four-dimensional gauge theory.]] so the four-dimensional ultraviolet half of every construction of the corpus's kind has stood on it as an assumption. It is not an assumption here but a theorem on this work's path. Its content is exact: his equations (0.18) and (0.20) define the effective coupling at each step by subtracting from the inverse coupling the coefficient β_{k+1} that his extraction (0.28) reads off the step's output as the marginal term, and Theorem 2 is the statement that this coefficient stays between two positive multiples of log *L* at every step, from which the existence of the bare coupling, the bound (0.31) and asymptotic freedom follow by summation and the intermediate value theorem. The coefficient has two parts per step, the background dependence of the fluctuation determinant of his Gaussian normalisation — the one-loop coefficient of the block scheme, whose continuum value for SU(2) is 0.2575 per doubling in Wilson's normalisation of the coupling — and a second-order part bounded through Theorem 1's own estimates; both are stated with their pages on the open ledger, with the falsifier beside them: for U(1) the one-loop coefficient vanishes, so the lower bound and the theorem fail, and the chain separates the groups at the ultraviolet end as Theorem 8.7 separates them at the infrared end. When the bound is proved here, γ becomes a number this work produces and the interval [0.2452, 1/γ²] has two ends of its own. Below that ceiling the first rung of the renormalisation step, the small-field split of one block of side two with its Peierls and Kotecký–Preiss conditions, has its constants re-derived on the open ledger (the block Hessian's least eigenvalue 3, the entropy 54.4 per plaquette, the large-field weight the exact one-plaquette marginal): the coupling at which that 