he Euclidean fermion block renormalisation (1989), the two large-field papers (1989), the classical N-vector series (1995–1999), and the bosonic many-body series with Feldman, Knörrer and Trubowitz (2008–2022); no paper on four-dimensional gauge theory.]] so the four-dimensional ultraviolet half of every construction of the corpus's kind rests on an announced and unpublished theorem, and every sentence of this paper that cites Bałaban's four-dimensional result carries that clause.[[fn:T. Bałaban, *Comm. Math. Phys.* 109 (1987), 249–301: Theorem 1 (p. 259) and Theorem 3 (p. 267), the recursion (0.20) (p. 256), the action (0.2) with the normalised trace (p. 252), read from the pages on 29 October 2026. The statement that the sequence can be kept small in four dimensions, his Theorem 2 (pp. 259–260), is deferred there to "a separate paper"; the four-dimensional stability theorem itself is Theorem 1 of *Large field renormalization II*, *Comm. Math. Phys.* 122 (1989), 355–392, p. 355, read on 29 October 2026: "If the sequence of the effective coupling constants is contained in an interval ]0, γ] with a sufficiently small positive γ, then the effective densities ρ_k have the form, and satisfy all the conditions and bounds, described in Sect. 2", followed by "Theorem 2 of [I] allows us to remove the assumption … The proof of Theorem 2 … has not been published yet, so we have Theorem 1 with the assumption"; its bibliography names no paper proving Theorem 2.]] Dimock's expositions treat the abelian and scalar cases and state every threshold the same way. Within the certified form the next widening is the same theorem in the Wasserstein metric of the three-sphere, whose one-link constant is 1/4, exact in the two symmetric directions and supported to one per cent between them, against total variation's 4/(3π); with the constants proved so far it gains nothing over Theorem 8.7, it is predicted to close near β = 0.30 under two constants not yet proved, and it is unguessed to the digit until they are. The tools that would cross the interval are named and not claimed: a finite-volume criterion with typical rather than extremal boundary data, for which no theorem exists in a non-abelian gauge theory; a correlation inequality, Griffiths' second inequality for untruncated SU(2) Wilson loops, which holds on the exact tube of §8.2 at β = 0.5 and 1.0, is undecided at 2.0 and 2.3, and from which, by the *r*⁻⁸ plaquette correlations of the U(1) Coulomb phase, no finite-volume criterion for truncated correlations can follow; and the centre of the group, whose one published every-coupling argument rests on a matching inequality that its examiners found established only where the high-temperature expansion converges, and which, as structured, would confine U(1) as well.[[fn:E. T. Tomboulis, "Confinement for all values of the coupling in four-dimensional SU(2) gauge theory", arXiv:0707.2179 (2007); K. R. Ito and E. Seiler, "On the recent paper on quark confinement by Tomboulis", arXiv:0711.4930 (2007), and "Further discussion of Tomboulis' approach to the confinement problem", arXiv:0803.3019 (2008); Tomboulis's reply, arXiv:0712.2620 (2007). Sources [22]–[23].]]`));
