+  push(Thm('Theorem 8.9', 'The lattice mass gap at strong coupling, extended by the flux-surface gas; Edwin Boston III', [
+    `Consider the SU(2) lattice gauge theory on ℤ⁴ with Wilson's action, each plaquette weighted by exp(½ β tr U_{p}), at 0 < β ≤ β*, where β* = 0.4764 is the largest coupling on the grid of step 2 × 10⁻⁴ at which the certified Kotecký–Preiss inequality of the flux-surface gas holds in the dressing of its page (in the tree dressing of §20 of that page the same inequality holds to β* = 0.4816). Then the polymer expansion of the theory converges absolutely in every finite volume with bounds uniform in the volume, the infinite-volume Gibbs measure is unique, and for all bounded gauge-invariant observables A and B depending on finitely many links, with d the least distance between their supports, |⟨AB⟩ − ⟨A⟩⟨B⟩| ≤ K_{A}K_{B}‖A‖‖B‖e^{−αd}, where α = α(β) is any value at which the inequality is certified (at β = β* the certificate holds for α in [0.0041, 0.0047]) and K_{A} depends on A's support alone. On the gauge-invariant sector the Hamiltonian H of the transfer matrix has spectrum in {0} ∪ [α, ∞) in lattice units: a mass gap of at least α(β).`,
